1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Every agent demo shows you what the agent can do.
This one shows you what it can't do — without you.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:20,000
A secret enters rysh exactly once — the moment you type it.
Even that line is masked before it reaches the buffer, or the history.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:32,000
It's stored scoped to this workspace — persist it, and it lands in a
folder rysh git-ignores for you. The only way to see the value again
is to ask for it. Explicitly.

4
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Before an agent may change anything here, it has to read.
Enforced grounding — the default — starts every run with mutating
tools locked.

5
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Exploration only, until the agent files a grounding report naming
what it read. And if it doesn't understand, it doesn't guess.
It stops, and it asks you.

6
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:06,000
The gate is open — and the edit still doesn't execute. It queues.
Here's the diff; here's the prompt. It's been waiting five seconds now.

7
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:14,000
It would wait all day — that's the point. Approve it, and only then
does it touch the file. Or reject it, with a reason the agent reads.

8
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Masked secrets. A grounding gate. A human approval.
One runtime, not three plugins.
rysh, at rysh.ai — onboarding design partners now.
